WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:12.240
Our human newborn, Augie Nelson's current
abilities are somewhat limited.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.160
He can cry, sleep, eat and hiccup.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.949
The turtle, also newly hatched, can crawl,
separate from her siblings and begin a vast

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:28.970
trans-Atlantic migration, alone without parents
or learning.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:36.320
Her brain has already formed every connection
she'll need for the great journey ahead.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.551
Seven months later, Augie is learning to sit
up.

00:00:38.551 --> 00:00:43.269
In a year or so, he'll be walking.

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:47.329
Another decade, he'll be walking to school
by himself.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:53.159
By that time, the turtle will have completed
a solo 9000 mile circumnavigation of the Sargasso

00:00:53.159 --> 00:00:59.350
Sea and returned to the shores where she was
born to build her own nest.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:05.309
The turtle's brain was designed to masterfully
navigate the ocean, but Augie's human brain

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:11.210
was designed to adapt to any environment,
play any instrument, calculate the existence

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:17.280
of the space-time singularity, navigate the
Atlantic by himself, if he wants.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.030
Everything he needs to learn these things
is already there.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:27.540
We're born with as many neurons and as many
connections as you're going to need.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.990
They can go from any spot of the brain to
every spot with as many connections as possible.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:38.110
That's a brilliant design by mother nature
because it gives us this incredible amount

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:40.530
of possibilities for the future.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.330
But there's a catch.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.770
That gift of possibility doesn't last forever.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:50.140
Like childhood itself, it comes to an end.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.710
For each skill, we get a golden period when
learning is easy.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:02:00.450
Periods in development when we have a heightened
plasticity for shaping neural circuitry and

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.860
we refer to those as critical periods.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:10.560
Learning to play the guitar as a child is
like opening a door and as a child, we have

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.270
lots and lots of doors.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.239
During this time, our neural circuitry gets
fine tuned.

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:21.590
Our brain learns about which connections are
going to be important through a use it or

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:22.970
lose it principle.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:29.879
Certain connections become reinforced and
sustained and enhanced at the cost of others.

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:35.319
Those connections that aren't being activated
because they're learned not to be necessary,

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:36.550
get pruned away.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:41.090
They actually die off, and the open doors
begin to close.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:45.109
As an adult, if you've never played, you've
lost that door.

00:02:45.109 --> 00:02:53.150
So right now, Augie can learn to speak any
human language perfectly without an accent,

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:58.190
but when he's an adult, he will have lost
that ability.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:04.110
If he doesn't begin learning the violin before
he's about seven years old, he'll probably

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:05.849
never play Carnegie Hall.

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:12.150
If you never had the connections to begin
with, you can dry as hard as you can.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.939
You're never going to get there.

00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:16.109
But what if we could change that?

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:22.709
What if we could tweak our brains to learn
as easily as a child?

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:28.159
It's called The Holy Grail of Neuroscience,
and it is tantalizingly close.

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:34.920
We can, to some degree, do the impossible
and reopen critical periods to say we allow

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.329
changes to happen later on, in ways that we
didn't anticipate would be possible.

00:03:40.329 --> 00:03:46.760
Scientists are learning how we learn, and
that may soon give us a chance to reopen those

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.760
doors.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:54.590
So what actually is neuroplasticity?

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.499
Neuroplasticity is the ability of the brain
to adapt to changing circumstances.

00:03:59.499 --> 00:04:05.010
To an environment changed, the ability of
our brain to in a nutshell learn.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.980
But there's another side of the coin.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.720
Just as we need to be able to learn to adapt,
we also need stability.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.790
The opposite of plasticity, neuroplasticity,
is brain stability.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:20.510
So in fact, that balance is what we're going
to focus on a lot tonight.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.420
Our first participant was the first woman
to chair the Department of Neurobiology at

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:26.840
Harvard Medical School.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.690
Today, she's professor of Biology and Neurobiology
at Stanford University.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:37.970
Please welcome one of the world's foremost
neurosciences, Carla Shatz.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:44.930
Our next participant is Associate Professor
of Psychology at Columbia University, director

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:55.820
of the Developmental Affective Neuroscience
Laboratory, Please welcome Nim Tottenham.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:00.500
Our third participant this evening is Professor
of Neurology at Harvard Medical School.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:06.850
An MD-PhD, he says his research falls in the
area between the brain and the mind.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:11.680
Please welcome Alvaro Pascual-Leone.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.740
So welcome everybody.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.380
So Carla, let's start with the brain itself.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:26.150
We're going to be talking about learning plasticity,
how the brain learns, how skills become habit.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.180
But what do these things actually look like
in the brain structurally?

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:35.180
We saw already some nice photographs in the
introduction, if you remember of these black

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:36.320
things.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.780
Structurally, the brain is composed of neurons
and support cells.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:47.030
The neurons communicate with each other through
electrical signaling; chemical electrical

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:48.030
signaling.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:53.520
The most important aspect of that is that
these communications are transferred across

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.020
structures known as synapses.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.540
That's where learning happens.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:05.470
That's where memories are stored, and as we
were talking about in the video, that's where

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:11.030
the pruning, this pruning process goes on,
this use it or lose it process goes on during

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.030
development.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.620
So, in fact, the structure of the brain is
just the brain is packed with these neurons,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.930
packed with these synapses and also with very
long connections.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:28.440
Some of those you can actually see in this
beautiful graphic that's being displayed now.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:34.330
So the brain connections link various parts
of the structure to each other, ad those are

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:38.940
the beautiful colors that look like Us and
so on, these pathways.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:45.940
But those are made up of hundreds and thousands
of connections from individual neurons.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:51.090
I used to joke there are more neurons in the
brain than stars in the universe, but in fact,

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:56.420
an astronomer corrected me and said that wasn't
true.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:01.510
So when you look at an amazing picture like
that, we're looking really at the axons, the

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:02.660
wiring-
The wiring.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:07.120
Is the brain fundamentally un-wired at the
start?

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.670
The brain is not fundamentally un-wired at
the start.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:16.190
But it is amazing, if you just take the visual
system, for example, the eye is not connected

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.670
to the brain to start with.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:24.300
The nerve cells in the eye, have to grow their
connections along pathways and select the

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.910
right part of the brain, namely the visual
part of the brain, not the auditory part of

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.140
the brain or the motor part of the brain.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:37.100
So the wiring from the very beginning is very
highly organized and the formation of these

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:46.240
long tracks or pathways is dictated by very
strict cues like pathways or even like roads

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.560
with street signs on them, so that these growing
connections or the growing axons follow these

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.450
pathways.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:08:00.620
But then the amazing thing is once the connections
form between these long distant regions of

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:06.590
the brain, then there's this period of overproduction
and pruning that we're going to talk about.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:13.140
A period of extensive plasticity where the
outcome is really predicted by the use of

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.920
the circuit itself and the experience itself.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:22.210
So there's both directed kind of hard wiring
early, followed by this remodeling plasticity

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:27.200
that happens later and really persists to
some extent throughout life.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.289
So then building on that, if we think about
it, if you're building a house, you're not

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:35.700
going to lay copper wires all over the place
and then pull out the ones that don't seem

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.460
like they're working well.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:39.190
But that seems to be what we're talking about.

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:44.680
It seems pretty inefficient and the brain
is 2% of the body weight, but yet gets 20%

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:47.440
of the blood flow and 25% of the glucose.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.779
So why is the brain doing it that way?

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:55.930
NIM Yeah, so on face value, there's so much
about brain development that seems inefficient,

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:01.490
but actually it's that seemingly inefficient
path that actually gives rise to the incredible

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:05.500
complexity of the brain, especially in the
case of the human.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:10.410
So when we're developing, what our brain does
is first goes through this period of over

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:17.210
producing our neurons and synapses and then
through experience, through learning what's

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:22.790
important in the environment, what synapses
are getting activated, those get to stay and

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:27.950
everything else gets snipped away because
it's inefficient to keep all of those synapses.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:33.279
So, why would we go through this entire process
of over producing and then pulling back?

00:09:33.279 --> 00:09:39.060
That's really at the core of what the function
of childhood is in the human.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:43.840
So if you think about humans as a species,
we're amazing in many ways.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:46.000
We can live anywhere on the planet.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:52.490
We can speak any language, we can eat a number
of foods and so on and live very well.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:57.000
Some of the reason why we can do that is because
of this long period of brain development that

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.000
we have.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.700
So the brain overproduces, it's like throwing
a big fishing net out into the ocean because

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.110
you're not sure which house you're going to
end up in.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:08.550
You're not sure what language people are going
to be speaking to you.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.840
So you're ready, you've got all your bases
covered.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:17.270
Then depending on what signals are coming
into the brain, the brain learns, "Okay, I'm

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:20.440
going to keep the synapses, but I'm going
to get rid of these.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:24.740
So if I'm growing up in a Japanese speaking
household, I'm going to hang on to the synapses

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:30.560
that help me understand Japanese, but get
rid of the synapses that support other languages

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.380
because that's really efficient."

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:37.530
So it really is this developmental period,
a really long one in humans, that gives rise

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:42.339
to our incredible adaptation to our particular
environments.

00:10:42.339 --> 00:10:45.870
So, Alvaro, is this a new concept?

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.920
We all think everything's been figured out
in neuroscience in the last 20 years, but

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.890
this need to balance stability and plasticity,
tell us a little more about that, about the

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.500
history of that conceptual.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:02.480
I think there's lots of new developments,
but many of the ideas are not fundamentally

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:06.310
new, we have the tools to actually test the
concepts.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:14.810
But the first person to speak about plasticity
and stability as a term and apply to behavior;

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:22.080
to human behavior, was Williams James; the
psychologist Williams James in the 1890s.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:31.110
He wrote about plasticity as being the property
for substance of an organ, presumably of the

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:37.830
brain, that enabled us as humans to do certain
behaviors and become really good at it.

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:41.510
He was talking about behaviors, a habit, not
about the brain structure.

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:49.750
He said, the amazing thing, it has a property
of giving in to influence but not giving in

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:50.750
all at once.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:54.400
You want to have some plasticity but not too
much plasticity.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:58.710
You want the right kind of plasticity, the
right amount of plasticity.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:05.750
Presumably, it was Ramón y Cajal, not much
longer, who started to look into where does

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:13.590
that actually happen in the brain and describe
the synopsis and the changes in the connectivity

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:19.550
and the change of how efficacious those connections
were in making new ones as the substrate for

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:22.500
that plasticity that Williams James was talking
about.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:30.020
So the ideas are 100 years old, and yet when
I was studying medicine, I was told, "Things

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:34.660
don't change in the brain and when you get
old, well tough."

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:41.510
I thought, God, I hope we can do something
about it because I hope to get there one day."

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:48.089
I think we start and all know, we've always
known, as an old dog, you can still learn

00:12:48.089 --> 00:12:49.570
new tricks.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:56.220
It was against the experience of everybody
that it is possible to learn new stuff.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.850
So how does that gel together?

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:01.360
Great.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:02.890
So now let's go back to this.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.920
We were talking about this concept of a critical
period.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:11.100
We all know the old adage that children learn
language a lot more easily than adults.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:14.720
But what are some of the other examples of
critical periods that you see in your work

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:15.760
with children?

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:21.680
So for a while, people were thinking that
there is a critical period of human brain

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:27.510
development that was the language that people
used and then it became clear that critical

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:32.040
periods are really a property of a developing
neural circuit.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:37.700
So if that's true, then maybe it's appropriate
to think of each neural circuit that we have

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.260
as undergoing its own critical period.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:45.250
That moment, the metaphor that people often
use for a critical period is that it's a window

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:51.589
of opportunity opening up when the environment
can really have its biggest impact on the

00:13:51.589 --> 00:13:56.940
nature of future functioning for that system.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:02.690
If it's true that every neural circuit has
its own critical period, then maybe we can

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:06.200
sort of map out the timing across the brain.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:12.970
So in general, what people find is that the
brain develops in this backwards C-shaped

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:18.750
nature where regions of the brain that are
low and back tend to develop earlier, then

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:26.270
the development curls around in waves.So what
you often see, sensory systems develop earlier,

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:32.560
experience their critical periods, followed
by motor systems and language systems, and

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:37.610
finally more of these higher cognitive functions
that particularly in the human, we spend so

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:38.899
much time thinking about.

00:14:38.899 --> 00:14:45.240
So, things like our emotion processing or
our cognition, academic performance and so

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:46.240
on.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:49.980
So, that hierarchical structure really makes
sense when you think about what the needs

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:54.580
of the developing infant child or adolescent
are going to be.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:15:00.120
So the video, we saw a baby turtle that really
doesn't have a childhood.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:05.580
It jumps in the ocean and takes off whereas
humans, we have this extended childhood period

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.100
where all of this has to happen.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:13.870
You could argue that because we have the most
to go through experientially, that we have

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:15.540
the longest childhood.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:19.930
I think it's another good example of what
seems like an inefficient design, right?

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:26.060
So if we're going to stay and mature for that
long, then we need somebody around us who's

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:30.140
willing to put in the investment to stick
around with us that long.

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:35.970
So if you look at this immature period in
the human, it's really a long time, right?

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:41.269
In most species, this period of immaturity
is on the order of weeks, maybe months.

00:15:41.269 --> 00:15:43.899
In humans, it's years.

00:15:43.899 --> 00:15:49.920
So that's a curious design from mother nature
to come up with because it's a huge energy

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:52.900
suck for a parent, right?

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:54.680
To raise another human being.

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:58.160
I don't remember, I don't know what the numbers
are, but someone computed how many calories

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:01.550
it would take to raise another human being
and it's enormous.

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:05.350
So there's gotta be a really big payoff.

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:12.160
One of the arguments is that the payoff is
that this affords us as a species, this incredibly

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:18.959
long period of plasticity so that we can do
all the learning that's necessary to become

00:16:18.959 --> 00:16:22.000
a very complex adult.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:29.750
So at the same time, I think what we're learning
is that there's this notion of development

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:36.110
and then you get to some level of maturity,
which I'm still hoping for, and then you have

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:42.709
a plateau and at some point things go wrong
and if you are unlucky, you start losing it.

00:16:42.709 --> 00:16:47.070
This way of thinking about it is probably
the wrong way to think about it.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:55.210
That, instead, we should think of us developing
our entire lifespan until we sort of die and

00:16:55.210 --> 00:17:01.800
that therefore plasticity, even though it
may be through different mechanisms and different

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:09.020
efficacies and working on different substrates,
but it's still there.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:10.990
It's still there throughout the lifespan.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:17.510
So it's not a critical period in the sense
that plasticity is done and now you can reactivate

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:18.530
plasticity.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:26.270
You can open opportunities, but the capacity
of change, balancing the stability is there

00:17:26.270 --> 00:17:32.040
for the entire lifespan, which is from a neurologist's
point of view or neurosurgical perspective,

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:37.810
a huge opportunity and reason for hope and
of interventions.

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:42.390
This balance between stability and plasticity
is really interesting.

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:51.559
If you just think about some of the systems
that have to form, then you don't want necessarily

00:17:51.559 --> 00:17:57.410
plasticity throughout life in a system, let's
say, like our visual system where we need

00:17:57.410 --> 00:18:02.790
to have a stable representation of the world
inside our brain.

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:08.750
Then we can draw upon that to make computations
to put together and to have perceptions and

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:10.940
so on.So it's really quite interesting.

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:16.070
So some systems really need to go through
this learning period, but then to become more

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.990
stable than other systems.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:22.240
So let's let build on that a little Carla,
because the visual system, your mentors at

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:28.380
Harvard, Hubel and Wiesel won the Nobel prize
in 1981 for their fundamental work on really

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:31.460
the first insights into all this concept and
the visual system.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:33.220
So help us understand that a little more.

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:34.220
Oh, sure.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:37.380
So, this is David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:42.210
My mother ... I used to call them Hubel and
Wiesel and my mother thought it was a person,

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:47.240
one person Hubel and Wiesel, till she met
them.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:57.970
But yeah, so these two wonderful scientists
explored the visual system and began to try

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:01.140
to understand how it is, well, here's the
question.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:05.679
How come we see with one view of the world,
even though we have two eyes?

00:19:05.679 --> 00:19:08.740
So both eyes have complete circuit.

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:09.740
It's like you have two cameras.

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:15.140
I mean, both eyes bring complete images of
the world to the brain, yet unless there's

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:19.370
pathology, we only see one view of the world.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:25.460
The answer to this as both in the wiring of
the connections and also the fact that the

00:19:25.460 --> 00:19:28.820
brain has to learn how to use both eyes together.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:32.040
It does that during a developmental critical
period.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:41.020
So the connections from the two eyes begin
to be mixed together as the connections move

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:46.590
from the periphery, from the retina itself
into the central nervous system.

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:52.150
The first binocular cells are actually built
in at the back of your brain in your occipital

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:53.150
cortex.

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:54.340
That's your primary visual cortex.

00:19:54.340 --> 00:20:00.510
But even though the connections know to grow
to the visual part of the brain, they actually

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:06.480
don't know how to tune up to make binocular
neurons.

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:11.990
One way they do that is to interdigitate the
connections between the right eye and the

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:17.380
left eye into a series of beautiful right
eye, left eye, right eye, left eye stripes.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.640
You'll see two images here actually.

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:25.530
So there's an image where there are white
and black stripes and those stripes are equal

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.280
in size-
On the left.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:28.530
On the left.

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:33.311
Then there's another image on the right where
there are little teeny black holes in the

00:20:33.311 --> 00:20:35.429
middle of a sea of white.

00:20:35.429 --> 00:20:41.570
Now what you're looking at, let's just say
on the left, every little white dot is the

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:43.730
size of about a synapse.

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:50.230
So one of these connections and you're looking
then at literally millions of these connections.

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:54.670
What you notice is that they're beautifully
organized in the stripes.

00:20:54.670 --> 00:21:00.490
When Hubel and Wiesel first discovered this
intermixing of the two inputs, which are essential

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:05.429
for making the binocular neurons, everybody
thought they were hardwired.

00:21:05.429 --> 00:21:07.980
But they did a very important experiment.

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:10.700
The experimental result is on the right.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:14.980
Let me tell you about it in just one other
context, which is really to talk about the

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:16.950
mystery of the cataract.

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:22.030
So you know perfectly well if as an adult
you have normal vision your whole life and

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:28.410
you get a cataract as an adult, you lose the
vision in your eye because there's a clouding

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:30.000
of the lens.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.410
Then a miracle can happen.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:37.830
That you go to the surgeon and he replaces
the lens with a clear lens and you see beautifully

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.490
again, immediately.

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:44.420
This replacement could happen after maybe
10 years of not being able to see well through

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:45.420
your eye.

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:51.490
Now, in contrast, a little child who might
be born with a congenital cataract or have

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:59.650
some other problem with vision in one eye,
if that cataract is not a corrected immediately,

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:06.210
then that child will be permanently blind
or have severe vision loss in that eye.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:07.300
So what's the difference?

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:13.350
My grandma had a cataract, 10 years worth,
corrected, good vision, and then the child

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:19.440
maybe has a cataract just for a year, maybe
even half a year, and it's corrected, the

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:23.770
optics of the eye are corrected, the camera
works, but the brain can't see.

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:25.610
So what's the difference?

00:22:25.610 --> 00:22:31.451
Hubel and Wiesel did an experiment where they
actually checked the connections between the

00:22:31.451 --> 00:22:36.740
eye and the brain that was shown in the right
hand side.

00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:44.270
The open eye, the white eyes connections take
over way more than their fair share of cortical

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:46.010
circuitry for vision.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:50.500
The closed eye has those little piddly black
holes.

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:55.960
This was an amazing demonstration, a very
important demonstration, in fact, the first

00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:58.730
of the use it or lose it concept in the brain.

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:04.400
That the brain connections require use in
order to be maintained and in fact even require

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.660
use in order to be formed.

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:09.430
So can you go into a little bit how you've
been building on that work?

00:23:09.430 --> 00:23:13.460
I think you've got a video for us of how the
brain does this in real time?

00:23:13.460 --> 00:23:19.520
This really illustrates a developmental critical
period that happens after birth.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:23.280
But actually, we were quite interested in
knowing whether there are earlier developmental

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:26.390
critical periods in the visual system.

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:34.720
What we discovered is that even before babies
are born, the eye is sending signals to the

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:40.880
brain to start to really shape up these beautiful
stripes and these connections in the central

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:42.440
pathways.

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:48.140
The signals are the electrical signals that
are being sent from the neurons in the retina

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.970
into the central part of the brain.

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:56.130
They're actually like test patterns, they're
at testing the connections, and the ones that

00:23:56.130 --> 00:24:00.110
are appropriate are being maintained and the
other ones are being pruned away.

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:01.970
So this is the same theme.

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:07.360
The video that you see now is a picture of
that signaling process in the eye.

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:11.940
So you can just imagine it's like phone calls
being placed to the brain really early in

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:17.290
development and every little black dot here
in this video is again about the size now

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:18.980
of a nerve cell, not a synapse.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:25.160
So it's a bigger scale, but you can see that
whenever ... when the cells become black,

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:28.930
it means they're placing the phone calls and
they're sending their signals to the brain.

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:36.789
So, what you're seeing here is neighborhoods
of nerve cells, all placing phone calls together.

00:24:36.789 --> 00:24:41.100
This is actually part of another principle
of development, cells that fire together wire

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:42.100
together.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:47.920
So this is a way that the eye can test to
make sure the connections are orderly in the

00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:49.480
target structure.

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:50.480
This is happening.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:55.299
So the brain is really jump starting vision
even before vision is possible because it's

00:24:55.299 --> 00:24:59.350
in utero, and it's before the rods and the
cones have appeared.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:03.860
Now that we know that this happens in the
visual system, it's been found that this kind

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:08.730
of jump starting and testing is happening
all over the brain during development, very

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:09.990
early development.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:14.610
You've been studying ways to potentially reopen
the visual system.

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:16.450
So tell us about that a little bit.

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:17.450
Yeah.

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:25.800
Well, the question is really if these windows
close, can you open them up at any time?

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:30.200
And really to try to understand how to do
that, it's important to know something about

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:31.700
the molecular mechanism.

00:25:31.700 --> 00:25:37.730
So what are the molecules that are opening
and closing these developmental critical periods?

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:45.030
And really in particular, what are the molecular
mechanisms that control the pruning process

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:48.920
itself, this selection process, which synapsis
should we keep?

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:53.780
How does the brain know that certain synapses
have been used and they should be strengthened

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:58.809
and other synapses have not been used so much,
so we don't need them, so we can actually

00:25:58.809 --> 00:26:00.460
prune them away?

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:06.640
So we can use animal models to begin to discover
those molecules for pruning.

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:12.090
In doing that, in fact, we found a number
of candidate molecules that we wanted to test

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:16.440
to see if they were important for this pruning
process.

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:22.480
We engineered genetically mice that lack these
molecules to see what would happen to their

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:25.100
critical periods and to pruning.

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:33.350
To our great surprise and delight, what we
discovered is that if certain of these molecules,

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:40.470
when they're not there, permit the persistence
of the developmental critical period in the

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:46.660
visual system and in fact pruning fails to
happen.

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:49.440
So this is illustrating two points.

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:55.870
One is that it is possible to continue to
extend a critical period for a longer period

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:57.440
of time.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:06.520
It also indicates the idea that brain plasticity
itself can be regulated in a very deliberate

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:11.549
way, and if we only knew the whole story about
the molecular mechanisms, we really could

00:27:11.549 --> 00:27:16.260
make pills and I could take a pill as an adult
and learn French without an accent.

00:27:16.260 --> 00:27:21.179
So that brings up an interesting point because
Alvaro is over there smiling and shaking his

00:27:21.179 --> 00:27:22.179
head.

00:27:22.179 --> 00:27:23.929
So as a clinical neurologist-
I've taken the pill, exactly.

00:27:23.929 --> 00:27:26.900
No, but would you want to take it right away?

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:33.320
As a clinical neurologist and you think about
that and we talk about the balance between

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:38.100
stability and plasticity and as you're taking
care of patients with strokes and things like

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:44.120
that, what do you think about in kids as ... Well,
'cause everybody's obviously going to, "Well,

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:45.510
we got to get these into humans.

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:49.980
We've got to figure out ways to extend these
windows for kids and for disease to adults."

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:52.140
So let's talk a little bit about the pros
and cons of that.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:59.320
Yes, I think it's a really important topic
and it is a double edged sword.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:09.320
So to have a great efficient plastic brain
that is able to get us to learn French or

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:12.031
English without an accent, it may be very
appealing.

00:28:12.031 --> 00:28:18.010
But at the same time to have too much plasticity
can come with a cost.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:25.550
If you have, not in a genetically engineered
mouse, but a disease where by design, because

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:33.060
of their pathology, the brain is too plastic,
is learning too fast, and that seems at face

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:34.560
value a great thing.

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:39.500
You're able to acquire skills that go above
the average one of us.

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:46.900
You can open up a box of matches, have them
fall on the ground and say 27 and be right

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:48.730
or 225.

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:55.220
You can learn a whole book of names and phone
numbers and we call those savant abilities.

00:28:55.220 --> 00:29:00.480
It's sort of cool and it's certainly partly
a joke.

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:07.230
But it comes to the course because what happens
to the brain is that normally it yields to

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:10.170
influence to the environmental change.

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:15.600
Then we stop and then we experience a new
thing and that new thing falls on to fertile

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.220
grounds, ready to learn the new thing.

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:20.640
But it doesn't get colored, but what we just
learned.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:26.280
The risk of having too much plasticity is
that the brain is change and then the change

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:31.480
falls on to the change and the change into
the change and the change ... it is often

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:33.380
a messy, noisy system.

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:40.730
We think that for example, diseases like autism
are characterized by excessive, too good at

00:29:40.730 --> 00:29:46.780
plasticity, that leads to a failure of pruning
and to a developmental disorder because of

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:47.780
the number of plasticity.

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:54.960
So to have too much may be a bad thing.

00:29:54.960 --> 00:30:01.840
So you've been studying that in autism specifically
to try to look at plasticity in the autistic

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:06.310
brain versus in the neurotypical non autistic
brain.

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:12.150
So what do you find, how do you go about teasing
that apart and what do you find?

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:19.500
You have to come up with experiments is it
worthy to show that people with autism can

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:25.100
be better at learning for example than the
individuals that are not autistic?

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:31.840
And things like learning a sequence of finger
movements that repeat themselves by doing

00:30:31.840 --> 00:30:36.070
it to figure out the pattern underneath the
finger movements.

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:42.720
Individuals with autism learn to do that faster
than those without autism.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:51.210
But if you have one pattern followed by another,
then they break down because they run into

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:54.919
interactions between the two things that they
are learning.

00:30:54.919 --> 00:30:59.929
At the same time, we want to look in the brain
and see what is changing in the brain to allow

00:30:59.929 --> 00:31:04.070
them to learn these skills faster.

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:13.020
We do that in a similar way, that we've learned
to do it in animal models or in slices of

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:19.350
the brain using electric stimuli to evoke
a response and then using trains of little

00:31:19.350 --> 00:31:24.730
stimuli to modify those connections and see
for how long they remained modified.

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:28.559
We use that using a technique called transcranial
magnetic stimulation.

00:31:28.559 --> 00:31:33.539
So tell us a little more about that technique
and how you're able to use it noninvasively

00:31:33.539 --> 00:31:34.540
to test something like this.

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:36.470
To perturb the brain transiently.

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:38.659
It's a bit like science fiction.

00:31:38.659 --> 00:31:42.279
It's still to me.

00:31:42.279 --> 00:31:50.360
You basically put a coil of copper wire over
the subjects head then as the current passes

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:55.570
through the copper wire coil, it induces a
current that goes through the skin and the

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:58.270
skull and induces a current in the brain.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:03.669
Maybe we can show it and it has a little explanation
that goes with it.

00:32:03.669 --> 00:32:08.590
TMS stands for transcranial magnetic stimulation.

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:13.970
It is a way of inducing current in the brain.

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:19.820
You need specific controlled part of the brain
without having to open up the skin and the

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:20.820
skull.

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:29.210
It turns out that when you apply repetitive
stimuli, then you're activating a zero grid

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:33.700
over and over in your control pattern, and
that changes that zero grid.

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:43.289
So that allows us to activate, probe, disrupt
or suppress activity in different parts of

00:32:43.289 --> 00:32:49.120
the brain depending on where we target and
what parameters of stimulation we applied.

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:55.190
So you can probe the brain with this technique,
see the response, apply a little train to

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:56.770
modify it.

00:32:56.770 --> 00:33:02.190
Then what you find in people with autism is
that the effect of that modification lasts

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:08.309
literally longer than it does in neuro-typical
subjects in those without autism.

00:33:08.309 --> 00:33:12.700
So Nim, we've talked mostly and now we've
talked about vision and we've talked about

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:14.210
motor system.

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:18.780
You study a lot more aspects of emotional
phases of development.

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:24.490
So let's talk about how you've been studying
that in these exact same context of plasticity

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:27.110
and stability.

00:33:27.110 --> 00:33:34.270
A lot of our questions are related to the
point I made earlier that we have this design

00:33:34.270 --> 00:33:38.020
of spending a lot of time with our parents
while we're growing up.

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:45.840
So we have been very interested in the role
of the parents during this putative sensitive

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:48.610
period for emotional behaviors.

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:56.159
So, we know since the time of Freud, that
there is this very strong association between

00:33:56.159 --> 00:34:01.270
early caregiving experiences and emotional
behavior later on in adulthood.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:07.580
But we don't really know at the level of human
brain development why that enduring link exists.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:13.339
Part of the answer may have to do with some
of these critical period timing and the influence

00:34:13.339 --> 00:34:14.339
of the parents.

00:34:14.339 --> 00:34:19.580
So we've been asking questions about, actually,
what good is a parent?

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:24.440
We know that parents are important, but what
are they actually doing on a moment to moment

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:25.720
basis?

00:34:25.720 --> 00:34:30.320
As I'm rushing my kids out the door to school,
shoving breakfast into their mouths to get

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:35.710
them out the door, what are those momentary
episodes important for?

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:43.590
But we also have the opportunity to ask questions
about children who experience more forms of

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:45.399
adversity early in life.

00:34:45.399 --> 00:34:50.169
The reason that we've been very interested
in this group of children is because this

00:34:50.169 --> 00:34:56.740
early adversity exposure is one of the number
one preventable risk factors for emotional

00:34:56.740 --> 00:34:59.440
difficulties later on in life.

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:06.720
We're just at the beginning stages of understanding
why early adversity in particular is so important,

00:35:06.720 --> 00:35:09.820
maybe more important than later adversity.

00:35:09.820 --> 00:35:15.440
Part of the answer is related to the concept
of critical periods that we're talking about.

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:19.420
So you brought up the idea of a double edge
sword before.

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:24.320
I think that there are many double-edged swords
when it comes to brain plasticity.

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:31.530
Another one of those is that brain plasticity
is not really good or bad.

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:33.349
It just is.

00:35:33.349 --> 00:35:36.500
It just opens you up to the environment.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:42.380
So when considering or evaluating brain plasticity,
it's also important to look at what's the

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:48.400
nature of the environment that the individual
is experiencing when the brain ... when that

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:51.170
window of opportunity is really open.

00:35:51.170 --> 00:35:57.470
So for children that have experienced significant
adversity, like caregiving adversity or what

00:35:57.470 --> 00:36:04.619
we often call psychosocial trauma, what we
have seen, well, two main things.

00:36:04.619 --> 00:36:09.670
One is that there's incredible individual
differences in outcomes.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:15.550
So some children show some really significant
challenges and others are thriving.

00:36:15.550 --> 00:36:20.260
So this is an individual differences story
that we really aren't very good at explaining

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:21.460
right now.

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:26.230
But the other piece that we've been seeing,
and this is true not only in humans but across

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:33.120
a number of species, is that early adversity
may actually be affecting brain development

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:38.710
on average by affecting the timing of these
critical periods.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:46.380
So there may actually be an acceleration of
the timing or the opening of these critical

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:47.380
periods.

00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:51.970
So if we know that, that's really important
to know in terms of intervention and prevention,

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:57.480
because if the timing of these critical periods
are shifting as a function of early experience,

00:36:57.480 --> 00:37:02.880
then that informs how we want to treat individuals
and what new experiences we want to give to

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:06.140
individuals as a function of these early experiences.

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:10.380
And that's why you like to refer to these,
as you said before, as sensitive periods,

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:11.530
more than critical periods.

00:37:11.530 --> 00:37:17.590
For the processes that we're interested in,
these emotional behaviors and cognitive behaviors,

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:22.750
that seems to make more sense to me because
they do seem to be more malleable.

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:26.700
In fact, that's why talk therapy is supposed
to work.

00:37:26.700 --> 00:37:33.280
Is that talk therapy is about teaching the
brain something new about interpersonal relationships.

00:37:33.280 --> 00:37:37.960
The reason, the only way that talk therapy
can work is if there's continued plasticity

00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:40.400
in some of these systems.

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:43.730
So we talked a little bit about autism.

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:48.670
Let's talk a little bit about schizophrenia
because they're another very complex condition

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:54.010
and there are questions about whether or not
schizophrenia may have aspects of a disorder

00:37:54.010 --> 00:37:55.849
of stability and plasticity.

00:37:55.849 --> 00:37:58.700
So, Carla, maybe you could enlighten us on
that a little.

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:05.820
Well, I mean, one main point to make about
schizophrenia is that it's not really commonly

00:38:05.820 --> 00:38:09.260
considered to be a developmental disorder.

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:20.820
Even though often symptoms appear late in
late adolescence or even beyond, the current

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:30.470
idea is that there's this extended developmental
critical period and this period exists beyond

00:38:30.470 --> 00:38:39.330
the visual system as Nim has mentioned.Therefore,
these systems that are maturing much later,

00:38:39.330 --> 00:38:48.650
including those that have to do with interactions
and with cognition and frontal lobe behavior,

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.670
all mature later.

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:55.020
So then the question is what are the changes
that have happened in the brain?

00:38:55.020 --> 00:39:02.640
Again, it's commonly now thought that this
relationship really between plasticity and

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:06.420
stability must be perturbed in some way.

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:13.350
When people look into the brain of a ... not
a huge amount has been done, but there is

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:20.420
consensus that there are changes in pruning
in the brains of schizophrenic patients who

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:27.680
have donated their brains for studies so that
we can actually look at those brains.

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:35.650
In human genetic studies we're looking for
susceptibility genes that might produce schizophrenia

00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:45.540
in children, some of the new gene candidates
that have been identified are known in animal

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:53.140
models where we study how those genes actually
work, are known to regulate pruning in the

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:56.200
animal, like the mouse developmental critical
periods.

00:39:56.200 --> 00:40:02.780
So, now you're beginning to hear a kind of
common theme that's emerging as we talk, that

00:40:02.780 --> 00:40:09.500
there are these critical periods that involve
a beautiful balance between plasticity and

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:18.000
stability, involve pruning and may have different
outcomes, not only through our own experiences,

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:27.780
but also when the balance somehow is disrupted
by some kind of pathological condition.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:34.250
let's talk a little bit about how do we harness
recovery of damage after injury.

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:39.460
You talked a little bit, Nim, about a traumatic
situation, but what about critical periods

00:40:39.460 --> 00:40:44.560
in childhood, when we get to adulthood, how
do we attack that problem?

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:49.980
Well, I think the way we're talking about
plasticity really say it's a huge reason for

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:51.320
hope, right?

00:40:51.320 --> 00:41:00.240
It suggests the fact that our brain can be
guided and the challenge is to learn exactly

00:41:00.240 --> 00:41:01.670
how to guide it.

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:05.790
What do we need to do to suppress changes
that are these double edge sword that good

00:41:05.790 --> 00:41:10.859
cause problems and to enhance others that
can benefit the subject?

00:41:10.859 --> 00:41:15.850
We have lots of interesting indications that
that is possible to do.

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:20.450
Ultimately, that's what physical therapy,
what occupational therapy, what speech therapy

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:21.849
promotes.

00:41:21.849 --> 00:41:27.280
Is the talk therapies guide the changes for
the benefit of the subject.

00:41:27.280 --> 00:41:33.030
But I think we have now as we understand more
of the molecular basis and the physiologic

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:43.250
basis of these mechanisms, we have ways to
target these mechanisms with medications or

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:51.369
with devices, with brain simulation that more
directly to the brain promote the changes.

00:41:51.369 --> 00:41:57.230
That's happening and it's happening guided
by basic research and translating that basic

00:41:57.230 --> 00:42:00.620
research to humans.

00:42:00.620 --> 00:42:02.329
So ... sorry.

00:42:02.329 --> 00:42:07.550
One of the things that I'm really excited
about in terms of intervention is thinking

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:13.609
about how can we boost the power of current
interventions by knowledge about critical

00:42:13.609 --> 00:42:15.220
periods in plasticity?

00:42:15.220 --> 00:42:23.911
So if we can increase plasticity prior to
some known effective intervention, that's

00:42:23.911 --> 00:42:25.329
really powerful.

00:42:25.329 --> 00:42:34.030
So, as you mentioned, there are many examples
in basic neuroscience where pharmacologic

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:41.510
or genetic administration or modification
can actually increase plasticity, and in many

00:42:41.510 --> 00:42:47.321
ways, bring the brain back to that childlike
state to allow for some environments to have

00:42:47.321 --> 00:42:50.150
a really big effect on the brain.

00:42:50.150 --> 00:42:59.140
That's really powerful and there's a demonstration
in humans too that has actually used one of

00:42:59.140 --> 00:43:07.780
these pharmacological manipulations with people
who don't have perfect pitch and perfect pitch

00:43:07.780 --> 00:43:11.119
depends on critical period learning early
in development.

00:43:11.119 --> 00:43:18.190
So perfect pitch was able to be developed
in adults who had taken this pharmacologic

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:19.839
drug.

00:43:19.839 --> 00:43:24.170
So then the question is, well, why don't we
all do that?

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:26.020
Right?

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:32.859
The authors of the study make a really ... they're
very careful to say that, "Hey, remember the

00:43:32.859 --> 00:43:41.070
brain actually spends a lot of energy to prevent
these critical periods from opening up again."

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:46.140
The brain wants efficiency, the brain wants
reliability.

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:54.290
Do we want to remove all of the lessons that
we've learned throughout our entire developmental

00:43:54.290 --> 00:43:55.290
history?

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:59.150
Do we want to remove aspects of our personality
and so on?

00:43:59.150 --> 00:44:05.369
So it becomes a very tricky question even
though there's a lot of promise for recovery

00:44:05.369 --> 00:44:08.089
following any type of trauma.

00:44:08.089 --> 00:44:14.710
But in that perfect pitch study, they didn't
... the people didn't lose ... I mean, they

00:44:14.710 --> 00:44:16.520
learned something new.

00:44:16.520 --> 00:44:18.490
So there's hope really.

00:44:18.490 --> 00:44:26.530
There's hope for having a selective intervention.

00:44:26.530 --> 00:44:27.630
I think that's exciting.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:29.800
I mean, from the animal studies.

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:36.410
So, from looking again at the mice ... and
actually one of the authors of that study

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:43.859
also has done work looking at the mouse critical
periods for vision as has my own lab.

00:44:43.859 --> 00:44:54.470
What we find is that you can actually go into
the adult mouse brain and really restore juvenile

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:56.990
plasticity in the adult brain.

00:44:56.990 --> 00:45:05.869
The mechanism of doing that seems to be, again,
to allow for new synaptic connections to form.

00:45:05.869 --> 00:45:14.470
If we study the story of the childhood cataract,
but now in the mouse, then remember I told

00:45:14.470 --> 00:45:23.120
you that if children have a cataract and it's
not immediately operated and, and clear optics

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:31.480
restored, then they become either permanently
blind or that vision is seriously diminished

00:45:31.480 --> 00:45:32.849
in that eye.

00:45:32.849 --> 00:45:40.380
And really there isn't a good way of treating
that blindness, is called Amblyopia, in adulthood.

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:50.190
But in these mice, one can actually make the
cataract model and then go in the adult mouse,

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:57.560
and engaging these childhood mechanisms of
plasticity a effect recovery of vision in

00:45:57.560 --> 00:46:00.140
the eye that was blind, which is amazing.

00:46:00.140 --> 00:46:06.220
It's really ... demonstrates, I think, that
by understanding the underlying mechanisms

00:46:06.220 --> 00:46:11.500
that regulate these developmental critical
periods, it's possible to go in and then to

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:13.589
manipulate them again.

00:46:13.589 --> 00:46:19.110
To me, it demonstrates another thing, which
is that there must be in the adult brain and

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:24.020
presumably in our brains too, 'cause we have
the many of the same molecules that are being

00:46:24.020 --> 00:46:32.480
studied in the animal models, there must be
brakes on plasticity because these manipulations

00:46:32.480 --> 00:46:37.820
essentially take the brakes off and then of
course you can worry about stability.

00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:40.160
So then we come back to that, this whole theme.

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:44.730
Again, but the idea that there's actually
more plasticity in our adult brain that we

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:50.850
can tap into, to me is really exciting because
you could say that even if there are some

00:46:50.850 --> 00:46:59.140
downsides, that after a stroke or damage to
the brain, wouldn't it be wonderful if it

00:46:59.140 --> 00:47:05.690
were possible then to tap into that latent
extra plasticity even briefly and combine

00:47:05.690 --> 00:47:10.099
it with training and physical therapy.

00:47:10.099 --> 00:47:15.359
So somehow a pill that would let me speak
French without an accent could actually have

00:47:15.359 --> 00:47:20.000
a really important therapeutic value, which
would be to really help people recovery from

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:21.000
brain damage.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:27.010
Well, what we have here is a video of a modern
rehabilitation technique with a split treadmill.

00:47:27.010 --> 00:47:30.240
Maybe, Alvaro, you can tell us about the concept
behind this.

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:31.240
Yes.

00:47:31.240 --> 00:47:37.670
So part of what physical therapy interventions
like this or like something called constraint

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:43.319
therapy that some of you may have heard about
it in individuals that have stroke, that cannot

00:47:43.319 --> 00:47:49.670
use both legs in walking correctly or cannot
use one hand correctly, we've come to learn

00:47:49.670 --> 00:47:57.790
that if you force the use of the other hand,
of the impaired hand by limiting the default

00:47:57.790 --> 00:48:03.720
of just using the unimpaired hand or by in
this case of the video, if you force the walking

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:11.940
correctly by aligning the paretic leg because
it cannot cross over, so, therefore limit

00:48:11.940 --> 00:48:17.930
the pattern of abnormality that normally would
happen, that because of this repeated training

00:48:17.930 --> 00:48:25.410
doing the right thing, that promotes the rewiring
that promotes the plastic change and speeds

00:48:25.410 --> 00:48:26.730
up the recovery.

00:48:26.730 --> 00:48:34.790
So you can, by guiding, by constraining the
behavior, use that as a way to improve the

00:48:34.790 --> 00:48:36.020
recovery process.

00:48:36.020 --> 00:48:42.599
So the opportunity of opening up the mechanisms
of plasticity in such a way that they become

00:48:42.599 --> 00:48:49.320
more active and therefore benefit from the
intervention more, becomes really appealing.

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:55.510
Examples of that do exist even in humans,
not only with medications.

00:48:55.510 --> 00:49:02.800
One of the things that we know about Alzheimer's
dementia, Alzheimer's disease is that before

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:11.160
the clumps of this abnormal protein come together
and deposit the plaques in the brain, they

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:13.190
are floating around.

00:49:13.190 --> 00:49:18.560
In that stage already they alter and damage
synapses.

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:26.160
So they become damaging substances for the
mechanisms of plasticity as it were.

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:31.319
If you take patients with Alzheimer's disease
and you try to get them to learn from video

00:49:31.319 --> 00:49:37.470
games or computer games and get better at
cognitive tasks, they derive very little benefit.

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:42.160
It would take too long to get a benefit that
is sort of meaningful.

00:49:42.160 --> 00:49:48.730
But if you activate that zero grid with brain
simulation, for example, just before they

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:54.089
actually engage in the cognitive training,
now you get more benefit from the cognitive

00:49:54.089 --> 00:49:55.140
training.

00:49:55.140 --> 00:50:01.770
So combining interventions, device base pharmacologic
interventions with behavioral interventions,

00:50:01.770 --> 00:50:09.340
I think is really a way to leverage what we
know about the mechanisms of plasticity in

00:50:09.340 --> 00:50:12.369
a way that translates into benefit for patients.

00:50:12.369 --> 00:50:19.040
So we've talked a lot about aspects of disease
and repair.

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:21.780
What about this Holy Grail idea?

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:25.940
What about the idea of actually enhancing
the brain?

00:50:25.940 --> 00:50:31.010
Whether one day I was thinking, "Today, I
had a long day," and I was thinking, "Oh my

00:50:31.010 --> 00:50:35.630
gosh, I got to focus on what we're doing tonight,"
and I jumped in a car and when is the helmet

00:50:35.630 --> 00:50:39.619
going to come out through the ceiling or already
being built into the roof where I can say,

00:50:39.619 --> 00:50:43.701
"If I can just get my temporal lobes going
and my memory working a little better, how

00:50:43.701 --> 00:50:45.700
great that would be."

00:50:45.700 --> 00:50:46.700
How far off are we?

00:50:46.700 --> 00:50:53.920
How far off are we from everyday enhancement
of function?

00:50:53.920 --> 00:50:55.690
It didn't work this morning exactly.

00:50:55.690 --> 00:50:58.060
You were in the wrong car.

00:50:58.060 --> 00:51:02.010
I was in the wrong car.

00:51:02.010 --> 00:51:12.040
I mean, what gave me pulse is that of course
there is a lot of industry investment and

00:51:12.040 --> 00:51:17.160
really direct to consumer devices already
claiming that that's possible, that we don't

00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:18.400
need to wait at all.

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:25.230
That you can just do it today and you can
build your own brain stimulator devices and

00:51:25.230 --> 00:51:28.119
then build it into your car if you want to.

00:51:28.119 --> 00:51:35.840
People will claim and try to convince us that
it is possible to enhance capacities.

00:51:35.840 --> 00:51:42.420
The problem with a lot of those data is that
what they show is that it's possible to get

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:47.859
better at performing a given task, not to
actually get better at the fundamental skill

00:51:47.859 --> 00:51:49.910
that underlies the task.

00:51:49.910 --> 00:51:57.829
So, I think that the attempt to get us there
is already here, but we need greater understanding

00:51:57.829 --> 00:52:04.220
including greater understanding of that double
edge aspect that Nim was mentioning.If we

00:52:04.220 --> 00:52:07.830
get better at one thing, will it be without
a cost?

00:52:07.830 --> 00:52:14.150
Will the brain allow you to improve something
at the cost of something else.

00:52:14.150 --> 00:52:23.900
So, for example, you can ask a question and
say, "What if we were able to get better at

00:52:23.900 --> 00:52:26.260
paying attention to this part of the world?

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:31.190
We know a lot of what the circuitry is, that
allows us to pay attention to this particular

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:32.190
spot."

00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:40.319
So you can actually identify those brain areas
in humans, apply simulation that increases

00:52:40.319 --> 00:52:45.720
activity in those brain areas, and you can
make people get better attending to this part

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:47.750
of the world.

00:52:47.750 --> 00:52:52.010
But when you do that, it comes at the cost
of attending to this part of the world.

00:52:52.010 --> 00:52:59.280
In fact, it does so because these two areas
sort of ... I almost say it is a yin yang

00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:03.640
kind of a relationship.So there are interactions
between the two hemispheres.

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:07.630
So if you improve one, you worsen the other.

00:53:07.630 --> 00:53:15.510
That's easy to test in something as concrete
as spatial attention for a visual task and

00:53:15.510 --> 00:53:22.210
something that's complex as overall behavior
or more higher order cognitive functions.

00:53:22.210 --> 00:53:26.410
What exactly the cost might be, is much, much
harder to test.

00:53:26.410 --> 00:53:32.220
I think it raises not just methodological
and biological questions, but real ethical

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:33.220
questions.

00:53:33.220 --> 00:53:37.869
If you want to improve certain skills, what
would potentially be the downside of it?

00:53:37.869 --> 00:53:38.869
What about memory?

00:53:38.869 --> 00:53:44.500
I mean, memory is something ... to varying
degrees, we're all here, slowly moving along

00:53:44.500 --> 00:53:48.579
chronologically and everybody worries about
what's going to happen to your memory.

00:53:48.579 --> 00:53:53.060
In my world, people have the ability to sometimes
put electrodes into the brain.

00:53:53.060 --> 00:53:57.510
That allows us to stimulate the brain and
there's a number of reports of different parts

00:53:57.510 --> 00:54:02.440
of the brain, if you stimulate at just the
right way, you can make somebody's memory

00:54:02.440 --> 00:54:04.890
performance better.

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:11.210
How do you think of that, as not a way to
combat aging per se, but to help people adjust

00:54:11.210 --> 00:54:14.200
as they get older and keeping memory intact?

00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:19.010
Forget about super memory, even just trying
to keep memory intact as we go along.

00:54:19.010 --> 00:54:22.450
How do you guys see us approaching that?

00:54:22.450 --> 00:54:30.579
I mean, correct me if I'm wrong, but aren't
there less sexy, innovative ways that people

00:54:30.579 --> 00:54:35.150
think about or have studied to enhance some
of these processes?

00:54:35.150 --> 00:54:45.050
So, for example, the things that we know we
should do, like getting good sleep, exercising.

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:51.700
Engaging in cognitive stimulation throughout
a lifetime have been associated with better

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:53.800
cognitive aging in the long run.

00:54:53.800 --> 00:54:58.490
So those are hard things that we all know
we should do, but routine things that we should

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:05.250
be doing that may actually be improving some
of this retention over the long term.

00:55:05.250 --> 00:55:08.940
So lifestyle modifications do work and do
work through plasticity.

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:15.790
So physical exercise promotes cognitive function.

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:20.900
In fact, if you do an analysis of all the
studies that have been done in the literature

00:55:20.900 --> 00:55:27.510
which we just did, just published in ... there
is a ... I don't know why this is the number,

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:31.630
but there is about 52 hours a day, 52 hours
...

00:55:31.630 --> 00:55:37.840
Talking about enhancing
It's really hard work.

00:55:37.840 --> 00:55:40.130
It's really hard work.

00:55:40.130 --> 00:55:42.460
52 hours in six months actually.

00:55:42.460 --> 00:55:48.069
So it's not a lot and it doesn't matter how
many hours per day you do it, it's sort of

00:55:48.069 --> 00:55:51.240
a total amount of dose almost.

00:55:51.240 --> 00:55:54.750
But you need to put in some hours but it's
not a ton of hours-

00:55:54.750 --> 00:55:55.750
Of exercise?

00:55:55.750 --> 00:56:01.690
Of physical exercise that improves cognitive
function in the elderly.

00:56:01.690 --> 00:56:06.920
So I think that there is an effect of physical
exercise onto the brain and it appears to

00:56:06.920 --> 00:56:13.800
be linked to mechanisms of plasticity, at
least as one can probe them in humans with

00:56:13.800 --> 00:56:17.800
brain imaging and brain stimulation techniques.

00:56:17.800 --> 00:56:25.970
But I think rather than getting at the level
of suggestions, you should sleep better, avoid

00:56:25.970 --> 00:56:31.109
medications that are bad for you, eat the
right thing and the right amount and exercise

00:56:31.109 --> 00:56:34.990
and challenge your brain with new things and
have social relationships.

00:56:34.990 --> 00:56:41.051
All those things are true, but we should be
able to get to enough understanding that we

00:56:41.051 --> 00:56:47.099
can prescribe it, that we can tell men, "This
is the number of hours of this exercise that

00:56:47.099 --> 00:56:52.310
you need to do and here's how we're going
to help you actually convert this hopes into

00:56:52.310 --> 00:56:53.310
realities."

00:56:53.310 --> 00:56:56.180
Because it's hard to stick with a program
like that.

00:56:56.180 --> 00:57:02.740
So I think that's the step that we still don't
have, is the understanding of how to actually

00:57:02.740 --> 00:57:07.250
make this a prescribable intervention, like
we do with medications.

00:57:07.250 --> 00:57:11.650
We don't tell patients, "Take something for
that."

00:57:11.650 --> 00:57:14.220
We tell them what to take and how much and
so forth.

00:57:14.220 --> 00:57:17.040
So I think we need that level of understanding.

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:22.309
But what about, Nim, happiness set points?

00:57:22.309 --> 00:57:24.890
Everybody wants wellness.

00:57:24.890 --> 00:57:29.660
Is it possible that we're going to someday
understand the brain well enough to say, "Okay,

00:57:29.660 --> 00:57:34.050
you've been through a really terrible life
trauma, but we can actually help your set

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:35.050
point."

00:57:35.050 --> 00:57:44.300
Well, I would think about sort of maybe not
happiness, but that feeling of being able

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:52.099
to regulate your emotions, to be able to feel
less sad when you want, to be able to feel

00:57:52.099 --> 00:57:53.530
more sad when you want.

00:57:53.530 --> 00:57:58.060
To have that emotion regulation ability.

00:57:58.060 --> 00:58:06.359
So there are studies, for example, with people
who meditate, which we generally think of

00:58:06.359 --> 00:58:08.310
as a healthy lifestyle practice.

00:58:08.310 --> 00:58:13.280
But there are different levels of expertise
in meditation.

00:58:13.280 --> 00:58:24.390
So there are Buddhist monks who do this for,
say 40,000 hours in total versus really, really

00:58:24.390 --> 00:58:29.910
dedicated meditators who might've done this
for 20,000 hours, and then novices.

00:58:29.910 --> 00:58:37.400
This research has shown that the amount of
meditation practice, practice being the keyword,

00:58:37.400 --> 00:58:45.130
that the behavior has to be routinely engaged
with, is associated with brain changes in

00:58:45.130 --> 00:58:49.940
the regions of the brain, like the prefrontal
cortex that we know are associated with emotion

00:58:49.940 --> 00:58:50.940
regulation.

00:58:50.940 --> 00:58:56.770
What's interesting in that finding, and it
relates back to the original walking example

00:58:56.770 --> 00:59:04.600
and the quote about habit, is that there was
this inverted U-shaped dose response function

00:59:04.600 --> 00:59:11.170
such that the individuals who were meditating
a lot but not to the degree of the Buddhist

00:59:11.170 --> 00:59:17.829
monks actually had the most different prefrontal
cortex activity and then it came back down

00:59:17.829 --> 00:59:26.240
again for the Buddhist monks, suggesting that
there is this element of plasticity in the

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:31.000
brain regions that we know are associated
with emotion regulation, but an element of

00:59:31.000 --> 00:59:37.850
habit can come into play as well, so that
it starts to become more of a naturally occurring

00:59:37.850 --> 00:59:43.380
state of the brain that can be, as you were
referring to before, the core process that's

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:48.170
being affected rather than the more superficial.

00:59:48.170 --> 00:59:56.550
So I would have to say in summary, that obviously
we have a really, really tight balance between

00:59:56.550 --> 00:59:58.819
plasticity stability.

00:59:58.819 --> 01:00:03.970
But as we've all learned, they're incredibly
exciting things, not just normal behavior,

01:00:03.970 --> 01:00:07.249
attacking diseases going down the road.

01:00:07.249 --> 01:00:14.710
But in the meantime, eat well, sleep well,
meditate once in a while, exercise a whole

01:00:14.710 --> 01:00:18.200
lot, and surround yourself with really smart
people who make you happy.

01:00:18.200 --> 01:00:19.880
For that, I thank all of you.

